WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.060
When you think about the money studios pour
into your favorite films, it isn't a shocker

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.350
that they may need to occasionally reuse a
prop or a costume.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.290
Still, it can be jarring to see some of these
outfits, which have been made famous once

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.389
already in classic films, pop up again on
some random extra wandering around in the

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:16.389
background.

00:00:16.389 --> 00:00:20.380
Here are a few times movies got caught recycling
costumes.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:21.779
Dress for success

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:26.340
Who can forget that fabulous purple dress
Clara Clayton wore as she, Doc Brown, and

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.720
their two weirdo kids ended the Back to the
Future trilogy by heading out on the ultimate

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.880
family road trip?

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:35.870
Well, it looks like she donated the dress
to a Goodwill somewhere along the way — because

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.969
the character of Shannon in 1992's Far and
Away came across the exact same dress in a

00:00:40.969 --> 00:00:41.969
Boston storefront.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:43.630
And just knew she had to have it.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:48.160
But while some dresses get to travel through
time, others just can't catch a break.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.370
That's one shipwrecked dress

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:56.210
After surviving the RMS Titanic's collision
with an iceberg in the 1996 TV miniseries

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.670
Titanic, this little black number on actress
Marilu Henner found itself right back on the

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.260
doomed passenger liner.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:08.750
This time it was worn by actress Tricia O'Neil
in the Oscar-winning 1997 blockbuster Titanic.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.340
Talk about a dress that needs a vacation — preferably
one that doesn't end at the bottom of the

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:13.860
sea.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.480
Of course, that's not the only outfit to survive
the Neptune's wet grasp...

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.710
Jacket Everlasting

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:29.330
Garments don't get much more famous than this
pink jacket worn by Kate Winslet in 1997's

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:36.140
Titanic, which sold at auction in 2016 for
over $160,000 and was actually made into a

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.140
doll.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.140
And yet even this iconic outfit was recycled.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.640
It popped up again, slightly altered, in the
2002 fantasy film Tuck Everlasting.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.170
Look guys, that jacket has a family to feed.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.530
No judgments.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.200
Helping Peggy

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.160
As we've figured out by now, period outfits
often get reused again and again.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.400
If something works for one 1960s series, it'll
probably work just as well for a movie set

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.460
in the same timeframe.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.730
That was certainly the case with this pink
frock, which Elisabeth Moss wore in the Mad

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.730
Men episode "Three Sundays," only for it to
pop up again in the 2011 movie The Help, this

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:11.950
time worn by Anna Camp.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.190
The hat of a hero

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.940
There's always a need for foppish hats, right?

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.060
Whether you're a musketeer, a pirate, or just
a guy who happens to like looking good.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:25.230
This dandy number first popped up in the 2001
flop The Musketeer, worn by future Grey's

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.240
Anatomy star Justin Chambers.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.260
Later, the hat weaved its way through the
Hollywood backlots and onto the magnificent

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.860
mane of Orlando Bloom for the 2003 smash hit
Pirates of the Caribbean: Curse of the Black

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:37.860
Pearl.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.440
It just proves that old expression: it doesn't
matter how bad the movie is — you just can't

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.140
keep a good hat down.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:45.140
Or...something.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.350
Prince of garment thieves

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.660
Alan Rickman was a standout in whatever movie
he appeared in.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:54.270
Take his turn as the villainous Sheriff of
Nottingham in the 1991 action/adventure Robin

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.580
Hood: Prince of Thieves.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.100
He single-handedly saved an ill-conceived
project simply by being capable of speaking

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.460
with an English accent.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:06.540
His outfits were no less extraordinary, particularly
this beaded piece of black armor that showed

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.630
up again in both the French film The Visitors,
and on the back of the Queen's advisor in

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.060
Braveheart.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.550
Fashion hunter

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:18.530
When you're one of the fiercest bounty hunters
in all the galaxy, you need gear that won't

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:19.530
let you down.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.770
That may be why Bossk — the Trandoshan bounty
hunter who appeared in a little movie called

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:29.060
The Empire Strikes Back — lifted his outfit
wholesale from an astronaut in a 1966 episode

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.290
of Doctor Who called "The Tenth Planet."

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:35.830
In fact, the suit originally came from the
Royal Air Force, who used it as a High-Altitude

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.380
Pressure Suit back in the '60s.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.550
Come to think of it, there's nothing more
high pressure than taking on an angry Wookie.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.990
Thanks for watching!

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.970
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.150
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

